Preserve nested relations across morphisms

Propagate nested predicates and function graphs through every
enclosing model. Restrict each morphism to its outer parents and
process each source's interior before its outgoing morphisms.
Topological order supplies every incoming fact before that interior
runs, so propagation needs no additional fixed-point loop.
Use inherited objects and morphism functions while keeping new facts
visible to rule evaluation.

Rebuild repeated-variable indices from full propagated tuples because
mappings can create or break the required equalities. Keep their owned
tuples and fact ages consistent with the full relations, and require
all repeated-variable equalities when inserting tuples.

Replace three expected-panic regressions with passing tests.
Cover function arguments and results, late images, mixed-depth
propagation, and inherited morphism functions. Check that an interior
waits for facts from every incoming outer morphism.
Cover repeated-variable predicates and function graphs, including
late images that identify arguments and copied outer model arguments.

